  "query_preview": "..To ring out the final day of the 2026 Monsoon \nseason, we will provide one last shot for precipitation to \nSoutheast Arizona. The upper pattern this morning is primarily \ndefined by a strong upper-low centered over northern Pinal \ncounty, and an amplified ridge of high pressure off the \nCalifornia and northern Baja coastline. Although the latest GOES\nprecipitable water imagery indicated that the deep tropical \nmoisture that was over SE AZ yesterday has been shunted east \ninto New Mexico, we are seeing some wrap-around moisture with \nPWATs AOA 1.00 inch across the western half of the upper-low \nfrom central AZ toward the AZ/CA state line. \n\nThis upper-low is the same feature responsible for the widespread\nheavy rainfall across Southeast Arizona Tuesday. It is moving \nquite slowly through the state today. In fact, it is expected \nto make its way to the east-central mountains of Arizona by \n30/12Z this morning, then into central New Mexico by 01/00Z this\nafternoon. There should still be enough moisture and instability\nassociated with the upper-low to result scattered showers and\nthunderstorms on its way out. Additional rainfall on the already\nsaturated soils of Pinal, Graham and Greenlee counties will\nresult in a delayed potential for flash flooding thru mid-day\ntoday. Therefore, we decided to extend the Flash Flood Watch in\nthis area until 11 am MST this morning.\n\nOtherwise, we will see the upper ridge off the coast of California\nthis morning strengthen as it builds into the Great Basin into\nthe weekend. As this occurs, it will force a surface high to\nmove south along the southern Rockies into far Southeast Arizona.\nThis will bring about some breezy southeast to east winds starting\nFriday and persisting into next week. The latest NBM probability\nfor sustained winds in excess of 25 mph across the valleys east\nof Tucson is generally 30 to 50 percent Friday into early next \nweek, with the strongest probabilities Sunday and Monday.",
